A frequency table makes duplicate letters easy to handle. Instead of treating equal letters as different choices, the recursion picks one character with a positive count, uses it, and restores the count afterward.

For `aab`, the first level tries `a` and `b`, not the first `a` and second `a` separately. This avoids duplicate results from the start.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void build(map<char, int>& counts, string& current, vector<string>& result, int length) {
        if (static_cast<int>(current.size()) == length) {
            result.push_back(current);
            return;
        }

        for (auto& entry : counts) {
            if (entry.second == 0) {
                continue;
            }

            entry.second--;
            current.push_back(entry.first);
            build(counts, current, result, length);
            current.pop_back();
            entry.second++;
        }
    }

    vector<string> permuteUnique(string s) {
        map<char, int> counts;
        for (char ch : s) {
            counts[ch]++;
        }

        vector<string> result;
        string current;
        build(counts, current, result, static_cast<int>(s.size()));
        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity is `O(n * P)`, where `P` is the number of distinct permutations. Space complexity is `O(n)` excluding the returned strings. The input string is not changed.

For the dry run `aab`, the counts start as `a:2, b:1`. Choosing `a` twice gives `aab`, while choosing `b` first leads to `baa` and `aba`. An empty string returns one result, the empty string itself.

This version treats the remaining counts as the state. If the same counts appear again, the cached suffixes can be reused instead of being built again.

The characters are stored in sorted order, and the state tuple stores how many of each character are still available. A recursive call chooses one available character and adds it before every suffix returned by the smaller state.

```python
class Solution:
    def permuteUnique(self, s: str) -> list:
        characters = sorted(set(s))
        initial_counts = tuple(s.count(ch) for ch in characters)
        memo = {}

        def build(counts):
            if counts in memo:
                return memo[counts]

            if sum(counts) == 0:
                return ("",)

            suffixes = []
            for i, count in enumerate(counts):
                if count == 0:
                    continue

                remaining = list(counts)
                remaining[i] -= 1

                for suffix in build(tuple(remaining)):
                    suffixes.append(characters[i] + suffix)

            memo[counts] = tuple(suffixes)
            return memo[counts]

        return list(build(initial_counts))
```

The cache has one state for each possible count combination, so it can use more memory than plain backtracking. The output still dominates the work for many inputs, with an output-sensitive time around `O(n * P)`, where `P` is the number of distinct permutations. Cached results and the returned strings require additional space. A string with all equal characters produces just one result.

Sorting the characters first gives the smallest arrangement. After saving it, the code repeatedly finds the next lexicographically larger arrangement. Equal letters are naturally skipped because the next arrangement operation works on the whole sorted character array.

This is iterative, so there is no recursion stack. The input `char[]` is a copy made from the string, so the original string stays unchanged.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<String> permuteUnique(String s) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        char[] letters = s.toCharArray();
        Arrays.sort(letters);

        while (true) {
            result.add(new String(letters));

            int pivot = letters.length - 2;
            while (pivot >= 0 && letters[pivot] >= letters[pivot + 1]) {
                pivot--;
            }

            if (pivot < 0) {
                break;
            }

            int next = letters.length - 1;
            while (letters[next] <= letters[pivot]) {
                next--;
            }

            char temp = letters[pivot];
            letters[pivot] = letters[next];
            letters[next] = temp;

            reverse(letters, pivot + 1, letters.length - 1);
        }

        return result;
    }

    private void reverse(char[] letters, int left, int right) {
        while (left < right) {
            char temp = letters[left];
            letters[left] = letters[right];
            letters[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
```

There are `P` distinct arrangements, and each next arrangement takes `O(n)` time, so the total is `O(n * P)`. The extra space is `O(n)` for the character array. For an empty string, the loop adds the empty arrangement once and then stops.

Here the result is built one character at a time. For every existing arrangement, the new character is inserted at every possible position. A map removes repeated strings created when the new character is equal to a character already used.

With `aab`, the first `a` gives `a`. Adding the second `a` creates `aa` from both insertion positions, but the map keeps only one copy. Adding `b` then produces `baa`, `aba`, and `aab`.

```go
func permuteUnique(s string) []string {
    arrangements := []string{""}

    for _, ch := range s {
        next := make([]string, 0)
        seen := make(map[string]bool)

        for _, current := range arrangements {
            for position := 0; position <= len(current); position++ {
                candidate := current[:position] + string(ch) + current[position:]
                if !seen[candidate] {
                    seen[candidate] = true
                    next = append(next, candidate)
                }
            }
        }

        arrangements = next
    }

    return arrangements
}
```

The code keeps the input string untouched. Because string insertion creates new strings, the total time is `O(n * n * P)` in a simple bound, where `P` is the number of distinct final arrangements. The stored arrangements use `O(n * P)` space. A one-character string and an empty string both work without special cases.

